  <h1>Antigen vs Antibody Test</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> An antigen test detects the pathogen's own protein and indicates an active infection; an antibody test detects the host's immune response and indicates exposure — which may be current, recent or past. The right choice depends on the diagnostic window.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Antigen test</th><th>Antibody test</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Detects</th><td>Pathogen protein</td><td>Host immune response (IgM/IgG)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Indicates</th><td>Active infection</td><td>Exposure (current or past)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Typical timing</th><td>Early (acute phase)</td><td>Later (days to weeks)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Example</th><td>Dengue NS1, H. pylori stool antigen, Strep A</td><td>HIV, HCV, Dengue IgM/IgG, Syphilis</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each method detects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antigen:</strong> a structural or secreted protein of the pathogen — present only while the pathogen is active.</li>
    <li><strong>Antibody:</strong> IgM (recent) and IgG (later/past) produced by the immune system — can persist long after the infection clears.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antigen:</strong> direct active-infection signal; fewer false positives from past exposure; detects early.</li>
    <li><strong>Antibody:</strong> broader detection window; useful for immunity assessment (e.g., rubella IgG) and late presentation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antigen:</strong> may miss low pathogen load; narrow detection window.</li>
    <li><strong>Antibody:</strong> cannot distinguish active from past infection alone (e.g., HCV antibody needs RNA confirmation); window period before antibodies appear.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>False negative / positive considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>False negative:</strong> testing before the marker appears (antigen too early, antibody in the window period).</li>
    <li><strong>False positive:</strong> antibody cross-reactivity or persistent antibodies from past infection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confirmatory testing</h2>
  <p>Rapid antigen and antibody tests are screening-grade. Confirmation uses molecular (PCR/RNA) testing or laboratory reference methods, depending on the pathogen.</p>

  <h2>Procurement considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Combined antigen + antibody devices (e.g., dengue NS1 + IgM/IgG) cover both phases in one SKU.</li>
    <li>Confirm the marker and specimen match the intended use before ordering.</li>
  </ul>

